    proseHtml:
      "<p class=\"lead\">A hot shot is an urgent, dedicated delivery. One truck, your freight, straight from A to B with nothing else on board and no stops in between. Road Linx Transport runs hot shot and same-day freight across Brisbane and South East Queensland for the loads that simply cannot wait for the next scheduled run.</p><p>When a job is down for a part, a customer is about to be let down, or a delivery has gone wrong somewhere else, this is the service that fixes it fast.</p><h2>What counts as a hot shot</h2><p>Hot shot freight is defined by urgency more than size. It might be a single critical part or a full pallet, but the common thread is that it has to be there now.</p><ul><li>A breakdown part that's holding up a machine, a truck or a production line</li><li>Freight left off a load that has to catch up before a job starts</li><li>A replacement for stock damaged in transit by someone else</li><li>An urgent customer order that can't wait for a scheduled delivery</li><li>Site materials needed before a crew runs out of work</li></ul><p>In every case the cost of waiting is higher than the cost of a dedicated truck, which is what makes a hot shot worth it.</p><h2>Direct means genuinely direct</h2><p>The reason a hot shot is fast is that it doesn't share the trip. There's no consolidation, no depot, and no other drops eating into the schedule. The truck goes to your pickup, loads your freight, and drives straight to the destination.</p><p>That direct routing is also safer for the freight, since it's handled once at each end and never sorted through a depot in the middle.</p><h2>Same-day across the region</h2><p>Most hot shot work is metro Brisbane and the Port, where we can move quickly. We also run same-day and urgent freight out along the corridor, to the Gold Coast and Tweed, out to Toowoomba, and north toward the Sunshine Coast and Wide Bay, when a run has to happen today.</p><p>Distance changes the timing, not the approach. It's still one dedicated truck going straight there.</p><h2>After hours and short notice</h2><p>Urgent freight doesn't keep business hours. Breakdowns happen at night and orders go wrong at the worst possible time. We run hot shot work early in the morning, late at night and across weekends, so a problem that lands out of hours still gets solved before it costs you the next day.</p><p>Short-notice capacity is something we plan for rather than scramble on. Keeping trucks and drivers available for urgent work is part of how we operate, not an afterthought.</p><h2>The right vehicle, dispatched fast</h2><p>A hot shot might need a ute for a single part or a semi-trailer for an urgent full load, so we send what the freight actually needs. Whatever the size, it goes with load-rated restraint and a driver who knows the fastest sensible route, and you can have a signed POD the moment it lands.</p><h2>Booking an urgent run</h2><ul><li>Call us with the pickup, the destination and how urgent it is.</li><li>We dispatch the right vehicle straight to the pickup.</li><li>We load and drive direct, with no other stops.</li><li>We deliver and confirm, with a POD on request.</li><li>When something has to move now, the booking is a phone call. The quicker you ring, the quicker the truck is rolling.</li></ul><h2>What it really costs to wait</h2><p>A hot shot costs more than a scheduled run, and people sometimes hesitate at that. The better question is what the delay costs. A production line down for a part, a crew standing around a site, or a customer you're about to lose all add up fast.</p><p>Put next to that, a dedicated truck going straight there is usually the cheap option, not the expensive one. That's the whole logic of hot shot freight. You're not paying for transport, you're paying to make an expensive problem go away today instead of tomorrow.</p>",
    proseStyle: null,
