    proseHtml:
      "<p class=\"lead\">Interstate freight from Brisbane usually means one thing for our customers: the run south across the Queensland border into Northern New South Wales. Road Linx Transport moves freight down that corridor every week, from the Gold Coast and Tweed Heads through Ballina, Lismore and the wider Northern NSW region. It's a lane we know well, which shows up as reliable timing and no surprises at the border.</p><p>Rather than handing your freight to a linehaul carrier and hoping, you get a direct run on our own truck from Brisbane to the destination.</p><h2>The Brisbane to Northern NSW corridor</h2><p>The stretch from Brisbane down through the Gold Coast and across the border is one of the busiest freight corridors in the country, and it rewards a carrier who runs it regularly.</p><ul><li>Brisbane to Gold Coast, one of our most frequent runs</li><li>Brisbane and Gold Coast to Tweed Heads, right on the border</li><li>Down the coast to Ballina and Byron</li><li>Inland to Lismore and the Northern Rivers</li><li>The wider Northern NSW region for regional deliveries</li></ul><p>Because we cover this corridor ourselves, freight stays on one truck the whole way. There's no depot transfer at the border and no third party taking over halfway, which is faster and safer for your goods.</p><h2>Crossing the border without the drama</h2><p>Moving freight from Queensland into New South Wales is routine when the carrier does it often. We run the border regularly, so the crossing is just part of the trip rather than a complication. Your delivery timing holds up whether the destination is a Gold Coast warehouse or a Lismore shopfront.</p><h2>Direct runs, not linehaul lottery</h2><p>A lot of interstate freight gets consolidated onto linehaul and passed between depots, which adds days and handling. Our Northern NSW work is direct. The truck loads in Brisbane and drives to the destination, so transit time is the drive and your freight is handled once at each end.</p><p>For full loads that means genuine point-to-point delivery. For smaller consignments we can combine the direct-run benefit with a planned multi-drop down the corridor, so several customers on the same lane share an efficient run without losing the direct-transit advantage.</p><h2>The right trailer for the run</h2><p>Corridor freight covers everything, so we bring the right trailer for each load. Tautliners and semi-trailers for palletised stock, flat-tops and drop-decks for oversize and building materials, and tail-lift trucks for Northern NSW delivery points without a forklift. All of it runs load-rated restraint and Chain of Responsibility compliance across both states.</p><h2>Booking a Northern NSW run</h2><ul><li>Give us the Brisbane pickup, the NSW destination and what you're sending.</li><li>We confirm the trailer, the timing and a price for the direct run.</li><li>We load, restrain to rating and drive the corridor direct.</li><li>We deliver across the border and provide a POD on request.</li></ul><p>If your business supplies customers on both sides of the border, having one carrier run the whole corridor keeps your freight simple and your timing dependable.</p><h2>Running the corridor both ways</h2><p>The Brisbane to Northern NSW lane works in both directions, and that's good for your rate. A truck heading south with your freight can carry freight back north on the return, which means neither leg is wasted.</p><p>So if your business also brings stock up from Northern NSW into Queensland, we can often handle both directions on the one arrangement. Two-way runs are more efficient for us, and that efficiency flows back into a sharper price for you. It's the advantage of using a carrier that lives on the corridor rather than one passing through it.</p><p>Because we live on this corridor rather than passing through it, we can hold dependable timing across the border and keep your customers on both sides of it supplied without the delays that come with depot-based linehaul.</p>",
    proseStyle: null,
