    proseHtml:
      '<p class="lead">Local transport is the everyday work that keeps a business moving. Stock from your warehouse out to a customer across town. A container stripped at the Port and carted to Rocklea. Three drops around the north side before lunch. Road Linx Transport runs local freight across Brisbane and the wider South East Queensland corridor on utes, rigids and semis, with the same driver on the job from pickup to signature.</p><p>Nothing sits in a depot overnight waiting for a line-haul truck to fill up. It goes on our truck and it goes straight there.</p><h2>What counts as a local run</h2><p>Most of our local work falls into a handful of shapes:</p><ul><li>Warehouse to customer inside the Brisbane metro</li><li>Port of Brisbane container cartage out to your yard or DC</li><li>Multi-drop runs around town, either one-off or on a set schedule</li><li>Factory and supplier pickups delivered straight to site for trades and builders</li><li>Stock transfers between your own branches or storage sites</li><li>Overflow when your own truck is booked out, in for a service or off the road</li></ul><p>If your freight is crossing town rather than crossing the state, it is a local job.</p><h2>One driver, start to finish</h2><p>Local freight goes wrong in the handovers. Every time a pallet changes trucks or waits on a depot floor, that is another chance for it to be damaged, mislabelled or left behind for the next run. On a local job with us, one driver collects it, one driver delivers it, and you get a signed POD with a name and a time on it.</p><p>It also means there is someone to talk to when the day changes. A site closes early, a customer pushes a drop back an hour, a second pickup gets added on the way through. You call the office and we talk to the driver, rather than chasing a load through someone else\'s network.</p><h2>The right truck for a metro run</h2><p>Sending the wrong size truck costs you money either way, so we match the vehicle to the load and to the site it has to get into.</p><h2>Utes and Vans</h2><p>For a couple of pallets, a few cartons or small gear, these are quick and cheap to run. They also park where a bigger truck cannot, which matters for inner-city shopfronts and tight industrial streets.</p><h2>Rigids from four to twelve tonne</h2><p>This is the bulk of local work. Enough deck for a decent load, still easy enough to get around the metro, and many of ours carry a tail lift so we can deliver to sites with no forklift or dock.</p><h2>Semis for bigger metro loads</h2><p>When the volume justifies it, 22 and 24-pallet trailers move DC to DC across the city in one hit instead of three. Both ends need dock access and room to turn, so we check that before we book it in.</p><h2>Where we run</h2><p>Brisbane metro, the Port and Ipswich are our home ground. From there we run local work around Logan and Yatala, down to the Gold Coast, north through Caboolture to the Sunshine Coast, west up the Range to Toowoomba and the Darling Downs, and across the border into Northern NSW.</p><p>Local means different things depending on where you sit. A Yatala to Burleigh run is local for a Gold Coast customer, and a Rocklea to Acacia Ridge shuttle is local for a Brisbane one. Give us the two addresses and we will tell you what it looks like.</p><h2>Standing runs and one-off jobs</h2><p>Some customers call when something needs to move. Others have the same freight going to the same places every week and would rather it just happened.</p><p>If your movements follow a pattern, a standing run is almost always cheaper than booking job by job, and you get a driver who learns your sites, your loading dock and the person who signs for it. If the work is genuinely ad hoc, book it as you need it. There is no account or minimum volume to get started.</p><h2>Early starts, dock slots and after-hours</h2><p>Plenty of local freight has to land inside a window rather than during the day. Shopping centres and hospitals with booked dock slots. Retail that wants stock in before the doors open. Sites that will not take a delivery while they are trading.</p><p>We run pre-dawn starts and after-hours deliveries as normal work, not as a favour. Book the slot and we will build the run around it.</p><h2>Compliance does not stop at the city limits</h2><p>Short runs get treated as low risk, and that is where people come unstuck. Fatigue rules, mass limits and load restraint apply the same on a twenty-minute metro leg as they do on a run to Toowoomba.</p><p>Our trucks carry load-rated restraint gear, our drivers work to NHVR requirements, and we understand where Chain of Responsibility puts the obligation on you as the consignor. If a load is not right to travel, we will tell you before it leaves rather than after.</p><h2>What local transport costs</h2><p>Price comes down to the truck size, the distance, how many drops are on the run, and how long the truck is likely to be sitting while it loads or unloads. Standing runs are priced per run once we can see the pattern, and they work out better than ad-hoc rates.</p><p>Call with the pickup, the delivery and what is on the pallet, and you will get a real number rather than a range.</p>',
    proseStyle: null,
